The problem is what I remember isn't a chameleon turning into
a popcorn monster then being easier to kill than other popcorn
monsters of the same type.  It's that I remember chameleons
turning into some sort of mean monster being easier to kill than
others of that mean monster type.  I'm often surprised at how
easy some red dragon was to kill then it gives a chameleon
corpse instead of a dragon corpse, or pick any other mean
monster type.

I've looked though "mon.c" and I think the answer is line 2469
near the end of this quote:

	/* new hp: same fraction of max as before */
#ifndef LINT
	mtmp->mhp =3D (int)(((long)hpn*(long)mhp)/(long)hpd);
#endif
	if(mtmp->mhp < 0) mtmp->mhp =3D hpn;	/* overflow */
/* Unlikely but not impossible; a 1HD creature with 1HP that changes
into a
   0HD creature will require this statement */
	if (!mtmp->mhp) mtmp->mhp =3D 1;

/* and the same for maximum hit points */
	hpn =3D mtmp->mhpmax;
#ifndef LINT
	mtmp->mhpmax =3D (int)(((long)hpn*(long)mhp)/(long)hpd);
#endif

However damaged a chameleon is during a polymorph, it gets
that percentage of damage to its new form.  It also gets that
many percent as its *max* hit points as well.

So let's say I fight it as a balrog and bring it down to 50% of
its hit points then it polymorphs into a red dragon.  It is a red
dragon with not just 50% of the hit points of a randomly
generated red dragon but it can't heal higher than that.  Each
polymorph, even if it's to the same type of monster, rackets
down its max hit points.  Each polymorph by the chameleon
locks in the damage you've done to it as it can't heal more.

Now I fight the red dragon a bit, ignore it a bit so it starts to
heal, then I attack it again.  It's down to 25% of the max hit
points of a normal red dragon when it polymorphs again and
just because I'm unlikely it's back to being a balrog.  Trouble
for the chameleom is, it's now a balrog at 25% hit points of
a randomly generated one plus 25% of the max hit points of
a balrog.  Every polymorph before healing fully and it gets
moved toward popcorn.

Thinking about polymorph traps in the mines monsters that
polymorph in them better have this problem.  I don't want to
have to face a balrog that gets low in hit points then runs
for the polymorph trap to turn into a liche near max hit points.
As it is when the balrog flees (having trashed me to the point
I might have fled) at least it turns into a very badly damaged
liche or whatever.  Without this racketing down I think most
who encounter a poly-monster in the mines could not survive.
That and monsters don't know to use a polymorph trap as a
healing spell ...


